1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,443
This is a short introduction to podcast generator, a tool that turns written scripts

2
00:00:05,443 --> 00:00:07,970
into spoken audio on your own computer.

3
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:10,951
It is also an example of what the tool can do.

4
00:00:10,951 --> 00:00:16,199
This video was made by podcast generator, from an eleven-page PDF and a narration

5
00:00:16,199 --> 00:00:16,653
script.

6
00:00:16,653 --> 00:00:20,800
Both are in the examples folder, so you can rebuild it yourself.

7
00:00:21,567 --> 00:00:23,848
Here is the problem it was built for.

8
00:00:23,848 --> 00:00:27,794
There are far more research papers than anyone has time to read.

9
00:00:27,794 --> 00:00:32,974
A short audio explainer, around seven minutes, is usually enough to decide whether a

10
00:00:32,974 --> 00:00:34,638
paper deserves a full read.

11
00:00:34,638 --> 00:00:39,448
And listening fits into time that reading can't use, like a commute or a walk.

12
00:00:39,448 --> 00:00:42,099
Podcast generator makes that audio locally.

13
00:00:42,099 --> 00:00:47,217
There is no API key and no cost per use, and your scripts never leave your machine.

14
00:00:47,967 --> 00:00:49,154
The idea is simple.

15
00:00:49,154 --> 00:00:51,529
A script goes in, and audio comes out.

16
00:00:51,529 --> 00:00:53,779
The script is a plain markdown file.

17
00:00:53,779 --> 00:00:58,842
An open text-to-speech model called Kokoro reads it aloud, in a voice you choose,

18
00:00:58,842 --> 00:01:01,342
and an MP3 lands in your outputs folder.

19
00:01:01,342 --> 00:01:03,279
There are three ways to run it.

20
00:01:03,279 --> 00:01:05,654
You can ask an AI assistant in a chat.

21
00:01:05,654 --> 00:01:08,404
You can drop a script into a watched folder.

22
00:01:08,404 --> 00:01:11,342
Or you can run one command at the command line.

23
00:01:11,342 --> 00:01:13,092
We'll look at the first two.

24
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:15,018
First, from a chat.

25
00:01:15,018 --> 00:01:19,501
Podcast generator includes a connector for AI apps such as Claude Desktop.

26
00:01:19,501 --> 00:01:23,319
Once it is set up, you ask for what you want in plain language.

27
00:01:23,319 --> 00:01:27,863
In this example, the request is to find a recent technical paper and make a

28
00:01:27,863 --> 00:01:29,680
seven-minute podcast about it.

29
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:34,103
The assistant finds a paper, writes the script, queues it, and starts the

30
00:01:34,103 --> 00:01:34,770
conversion.

31
00:01:34,770 --> 00:01:39,435
A few minutes later it reports that the audio is ready, and where to find it.

32
00:01:39,435 --> 00:01:43,919
You should still check the summary against the paper before relying on it.

33
00:01:43,919 --> 00:01:48,342
The point is to help you decide what to read, not to replace the reading.

34
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:51,039
This is what a script looks like.

35
00:01:51,039 --> 00:01:52,861
Ordinary paragraphs are spoken.

36
00:01:52,861 --> 00:01:57,621
Everything else is silent: headers, lines such as the voice and the link, and the

37
00:01:57,621 --> 00:01:59,619
source note in italics at the end.

38
00:01:59,619 --> 00:02:04,555
That lets you keep the citation and your own notes in the file without hearing them.

39
00:02:04,555 --> 00:02:06,024
One line picks the voice.

40
00:02:06,024 --> 00:02:10,843
As an option, a different line turns the script into a conversation between two or

41
00:02:10,843 --> 00:02:13,253
more speakers, each with their own voice.

42
00:02:13,253 --> 00:02:17,425
And a small lexicon file fixes the pronunciation of acronyms and names.

43
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:23,146
The second way to run it is the watch folder, which is the easiest way to work from

44
00:02:23,146 --> 00:02:23,623
a phone.

45
00:02:23,623 --> 00:02:28,628
On Windows, a small app called Podcast Watch sets up a scheduled task that checks an

46
00:02:28,628 --> 00:02:29,462
inputs folder.

47
00:02:29,462 --> 00:02:32,144
Point that folder at a cloud-synced location.

48
00:02:32,144 --> 00:02:35,063
Then you can save a script there from any device.

49
00:02:35,063 --> 00:02:39,950
The watch converts whatever is new, moves the script to an archive, and writes the

50
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:43,525
audio to the outputs folder, which syncs back to your phone.

51
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:45,872
Audio has a limit, though.

52
00:02:45,872 --> 00:02:48,592
Podcasts work well for surveying most papers.

53
00:02:48,592 --> 00:02:53,005
They work poorly when the argument lives in equations, charts, or tables.

54
00:02:53,005 --> 00:02:56,994
The page shown here comes from a briefing on a mathematical paper.

55
00:02:56,994 --> 00:03:00,198
Hearing that derivation read aloud is hard to follow.

56
00:03:00,198 --> 00:03:03,039
Seeing it while it is explained is much easier.

57
00:03:03,039 --> 00:03:07,996
For papers like that, podcast generator can make a narrated briefing: a video that

58
00:03:07,996 --> 00:03:11,200
shows each page of a PDF while its narration is read.

59
00:03:11,967 --> 00:03:16,182
A briefing needs two things: a PDF, and a script with page markers.

60
00:03:16,182 --> 00:03:18,510
One line in the script names the PDF.

61
00:03:18,510 --> 00:03:23,543
Then a line reading page one, page two, and so on, starts the narration for each

62
00:03:23,543 --> 00:03:23,858
page.

63
00:03:23,858 --> 00:03:28,954
The result is a high-definition video, with subtitles, and a small file recording

64
00:03:28,954 --> 00:03:30,464
the timing of each page.

65
00:03:30,464 --> 00:03:32,477
The rules are strict on purpose.

66
00:03:32,477 --> 00:03:37,511
If a marker is missing, or names a page the PDF doesn't have, the tool stops and

67
00:03:37,511 --> 00:03:38,895
tells you what to fix.

68
00:03:38,895 --> 00:03:44,117
It will not guess, so you never get a video where the words and the pages disagree.

69
00:03:44,867 --> 00:03:46,789
There are two ways to make one.

70
00:03:46,789 --> 00:03:51,812
The easiest is to ask your AI assistant for a narrated briefing video of a paper.

71
00:03:51,812 --> 00:03:56,835
The assistant builds the slides as a PDF, writes the narration for each page, and

72
00:03:56,835 --> 00:03:59,191
makes the video through the connector.

73
00:03:59,191 --> 00:04:01,485
Or you can build the pieces yourself.

74
00:04:01,485 --> 00:04:04,896
Export your slides to PDF, from PowerPoint for example.

75
00:04:04,896 --> 00:04:09,857
Write the narration, with a page marker before each page's text, or pull it from

76
00:04:09,857 --> 00:04:12,089
your speaker notes with one command.

77
00:04:12,089 --> 00:04:17,298
Then run the command, or drop the script and the PDF into the watch folder together.

78
00:04:17,298 --> 00:04:19,964
The preview option checks the timing first.

79
00:04:19,964 --> 00:04:25,173
Either way, there is a one-time setup: install the briefing extra, and make sure the

80
00:04:25,173 --> 00:04:27,592
free video tool F F M peg is available.

81
00:04:28,367 --> 00:04:31,043
Before you install, here is what you need.

82
00:04:31,043 --> 00:04:34,101
First, Python, version three point ten or later.

83
00:04:34,101 --> 00:04:39,326
Python does not come with Claude or Claude Desktop, so install it yourself, or use

84
00:04:39,326 --> 00:04:42,958
an installer such as U V, which can fetch Python for you.

85
00:04:42,958 --> 00:04:48,119
Second, about two gigabytes of disk space for the speech model and its libraries.

86
00:04:48,119 --> 00:04:52,197
It runs on an ordinary processor, so no graphics card is needed.

87
00:04:52,197 --> 00:04:57,167
Third, F F M peg, which is usually needed for MP3 output and always needed for

88
00:04:57,167 --> 00:04:58,378
narrated briefings.

89
00:04:58,378 --> 00:05:02,902
The first run needs an internet connection to download the voice model.

90
00:05:02,902 --> 00:05:05,260
After that, everything works offline.

91
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:10,548
The Podcast Watch app is for Windows, though the command line runs on other systems

92
00:05:10,548 --> 00:05:10,803
too.

93
00:05:10,803 --> 00:05:16,092
And you only need Claude Desktop, or a similar app, if you want the chat connector.

94
00:05:16,867 --> 00:05:20,206
To get started, install podcast generator with pip.

95
00:05:20,206 --> 00:05:25,247
Add the optional extras if you want the chat connector or narrated briefings.

96
00:05:25,247 --> 00:05:30,681
Then try the examples folder, which holds the script and PDF behind this video, and

97
00:05:30,681 --> 00:05:32,252
a sample podcast script.

98
00:05:32,252 --> 00:05:37,031
The read me file is a quick start, and the manual covers everything else.

99
00:05:37,031 --> 00:05:40,567
The project is open source, at the address shown here.

